encyclopedia :linked_slip do
  name "Pairing"
  kind :phenomenon
  subkind :physical_phenomenon
  status :complete
  topics :"fluid-reality", :danger, :"ring-era", :mystery
  prevalence :rare
  appears_when all: { place: [:displacement_zone] }
  summary "Pairing is the simultaneous displacement of two surviving sections of elven fabric in equal and opposite directions, even across districts or fragments. Zone wardens keep partner registers because movement or interference at one room can reach the other."
  medium "Paired elven fabric in the displacement zones — chambers and sections whose position-keeping was built to reconcile jointly, and still does, wherever both halves of a pairing survive"
  nature "A conserved exchange: displacement arrives in the pair as equal and opposite movement — one room's drift, turn, or insertion answered by its partner's inverse — the two reconciling like linked accounts whatever distance separates them"
  hazard "The pairing transmits trouble as faithfully as balance — a slip forced or triggered at one end arrives at the other on the partner's schedule, in a district that felt neither cause nor warning"
  known_forms "Charted stable pairs with repeatable matched movement; orphan slips whose missing partners leave irregular displacement; and chained events in which movement passes through several surviving pairs"

  descriptive_identity(
    signs: "The room moves smoothly with clean boundaries and no preceding " \
           "local disturbance; its registered partner records simultaneous " \
           "movement of the same extent in the opposite direction.",
    effects: "The pair moves in balance — drift answered by inverse drift, " \
             "gain by loss — and the two districts' wardens compare logs " \
             "afterward that match, line for line, with the signs reversed.",
    hazards: "A forced or accidental slip at one end can move the partner " \
             "without local warning; current registers let wardens warn the " \
             "second district and clear both rooms together."
  )

  prose <<~PROSE
    Pairing moves two chambers at once. One room's drift, turn, or insertion is matched by equal movement in the opposite direction at its partner, sometimes districts or fragments away. Warden logs from both ends match in time and extent with their directions reversed. Surviving paired sections are found in elven fabric whose position-keeping systems were joined; the link continues across the present distance between the fragments.

    Every displacement-zone register carries a partner column, exchanged between districts each season. When one room slips, its wardens warn the listed partner district before entering either chamber. Stable pairs can be scheduled around and some storage operators use their repeatable balance. Forced movement at one end also reaches the other, so work on a paired room requires both districts to clear their sites. Orphan slips, whose partners were lost with other fragments, move irregularly and remain under watch. In rare chained events, one slip passes through several paired sections; afterward the services compare times and movements to extend the network map.
  PROSE

  cue "The parlor slips with the smooth, clean-edged movement the wardens call bookkept, and the district office's first act is the partner column — then the courtesy call two fragments over: your entry just posted."
  cue "The two wardens lay their logs side by side at the season comparison — drift for inverse drift, line for reversed line — and initial the match, one more season of the old fabric keeping its books."
  affordance "A charted movement at one end forecasts the partner's direction and extent, allowing both districts to clear rooms and coordinate storage or survey work."
  pressure "Work that forces one room to move can displace its partner without local warning, so paired sites require joint clearance and current cross-district registers."
  variation "Stable pairs are logged like tides; orphan slips with lost partners move strangely and are watched; and the chained events that run whole pairing networks are the zones' great catastrophes, mapped afterward and drilled against since."
  variation "Pairings ignore present-day borders — partners sit in different districts, jurisdictions, and fragments — and the cross-zone register comparisons each season are one of the quiet arrangements the displacement authorities keep above all their other politics."
end
